From 18c890ae3fb27b39d781672d8afce1e942503371 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 03:35:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 159/159] hir_expand: nested coroutines follow the enclosing
 item's generics

Two failures in closures.cpp's coroutine (async block / generator)
extraction, both about which generics a coroutine's types name.

1. sad 0.4.32 subprocess.rs, `stream_into(.., writer: impl AsyncWrite,
   stream: impl Stream)` with `try_unfold(.., |mut s| async { .. })`:

       Lower MIR: common.cpp:688 Type param erased$1/*M:1*/ out of range
       for (max 0)

   An extracted coroutine nested in a closure or in another coroutine
   kept its `m_obj_path` and `m_state_data_type` in the generics of the
   item that defined it. MIR lowering of the enclosing closure or
   coroutine body, whose own generics are impl-level, then met the
   method generics. Nested closures already handle this: the enclosing
   rewrite (ExprVisitor_Mutate / ExprVisitor_GeneratorRewrite) remaps
   their path, and the type fixup reads the untouched
   `m_obj_path_base`. Coroutines had neither, so even
   `async fn f<T>(x: T) -> T { async move { x }.await }` failed.
   Impl-level generics only worked while the enclosing coroutine
   happened to number them the same.

   ExprNode_Generator and ExprNode_AsyncBlock get `m_obj_path_base`,
   set at extraction. The fixup builds their node types from it, and
   both rewrite visitors remap the path and state type of an extracted
   nested coroutine.

2. tokio-util 0.7.19 sync/mpsc.rs:78 (ast-grep 0.45.3), in `impl<T>`:

       Self(ReusableBoxFuture::new(async { unreachable!() }))

       closures.cpp:768 get_type would add a new param after freeze - T

   The block's Output (`Result<OwnedPermit<T>, ..>`) names `T` and the
   body doesn't. The coroutine's generics were frozen, and copied into
   its structs, before the Output was monomorphised. The async block's
   Output, and a generator's resume/yield/return types, are now
   computed before `add_bounds` and the freeze, and reused. They use
   the closure signature's order, monomorphise and then fix up. 0155
   fixed up first and then monomorphised, which mapped the paths of
   nested items twice. The generator's `Yield` associated type now also
   uses the fixed-up type.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
